 view is that you  need as much information as you can get  you want to look at the pharmacokinetics  you can't look at you can't just assume  that you have a picture of the  pharmacokinetics because you have the  cytochrome p450 genotype because there  are so many other things that can impact  the pharmacokinetics diet  the body mass and body distribution then  any drug-drug interaction so those are  all pieces that have to be taken into  account for that specific interment it  individual to assess the pharmacokinetic  result and then same with the biomarker  so I think that getting the oncologist  or provider the most information they  can get both in terms of genotyping and  the phenotyping with pharmacokinetics  and the biomarkers that gives them  information to interpret the specific  individual clinical presentation so I  think that you're absolutely right that  you need to to use the you need to take  these I don't want to say with a grain  of salt but you have to take this  information as something that can help  and not just be plugged into an  algorithm and says oh I have this  genotype this pharmacokinetic number  therefore I make this clinical decision  there is absolutely what you know from a  laboratory perspective it's not our  intention to take any of the judgement  away from the provider because they're  the ones who have the direct interaction  with a specific individual patient and  they're the ones who have to make the  decision so I think you cannot  generalize it and you must take that  information and use it as a tool to  individualize therapy for a specific  patient okay if there are no more  questions feel free to email me at any  time and i'll be happy to interact with  you offline thank you so much  oh I apologize we didn't finish it I do  need to put up the educational credit  link if you would like to get the CME  credit or the CT credit please go to the  bio conference alive calm website thank  you very much
 

